---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../components/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import CaseStudyHero from '../../components/CaseStudyHero.astro';
import CaseStudySection from '../../components/case-study/CaseStudySection.astro';
import CaseStudySubsection from '../../components/case-study/CaseStudySubsection.astro';

const sections = [
    { id: 'overview', label: 'Overview' },
    { id: 'problem', label: 'Problem' },
    { id: 'constraints', label: 'Constraints' },
    { id: 'exploration', label: 'Exploration' },
    { id: 'decision', label: 'Decision' },
    { id: 'solution', label: 'Solution' },
    { id: 'outcome', label: 'Outcome' },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout title="Lingotune — Julia Heo" sections={sections}>
	<CaseStudyHero
		logo="/images/lingotune/logo.png"
		logoAlt="Lingotune"
		headline="Redesigning song navigation for easier discoverability"
		role="Product Design Intern"
		timeframe="May – August 2025"
		tags={['Product Design', 'Mobile', 'EdTech']}
	/>

	<CaseStudySection id="overview" title="Overview">
	<h2 class="section-tagline">Users saw one song. Lingotune had an entire library.</h2>
		<p>
    <a href="https://www.lingotune.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="inline-link">Lingotune</a> teaches languages through music, pairing songs with short lessons designed to make vocabulary and grammar easier to remember.
		</p>
		<p>
			Users were landing on Lingotune’s song lesson page, seeing one card, and thinking that was it. In under a week, I worked with another designer and the founder to rethink the browsing interaction around what users were already trying to do. The update shipped in the next release.
		</p>
		
		<img src="/images/lingotune/oldold.png" alt="..." class="case-study-image case-study-image--phone" />
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection id="problem" title="Problem">
		<h2 class="section-tagline">More songs existed, but the interface didn’t make that clear.</h2>
		<p>The original screen showed one song at a time, with arrows to browse. But after reviewing ~20 UXCam sessions, I kept seeing the same disconnect:</p>
		<div class="observation">
    <span class="observation__label">01</span>
    <div class="observation__body">
        <p class="observation__title">Users were trying to swipe</p>
        <p class="observation__text">They reached for a familiar gesture before using the arrows.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="observation">
    <span class="observation__label">02</span>
    <div class="observation__body">
        <p class="observation__title">Users stopped at the first song</p>
        <p class="observation__text">The arrows were easy to miss, and nothing else on the screen matched the browsing patterns users already knew. Without a clear cue to keep going, many assumed the first song was all there was.</p>
    </div>
	
</div>
		<img src="/images/lingotune/user-test-review.png" alt="..." class="case-study-image" />
			<p> 💡 At first, I thought the arrows needed fixing. But the bigger issue was that users didn’t realize there was a collection to browse at all.</p>

	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection id="constraints" title="Constraints">
    <h2 class="section-tagline">Move fast, without rebuilding the experience.</h2>

    <div class="constraint-grid">
        <div class="constraint-card">
            <p class="constraint-card__label">⏳ &lt; 1 week</p>
            <p class="constraint-card__text">Users were already raising issues, so the turnaround needed to be quick.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="constraint-card">
            <p class="constraint-card__label"> 🎶 Keep the existing song cards</p>
            <p class="constraint-card__text">The solution had to work around the current content structure rather than redesigning it from scratch.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="constraint-card">
            <p class="constraint-card__label"> 🗒️ Work within the existing design system</p>
            <p class="constraint-card__text">New patterns still had to feel native to Lingotune, so I designed within the product's existing components and visual language rather than introducing a totally new system.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="constraint-card">
            <p class="constraint-card__label"> 👤👤👤 Small team, fast decisions</p>
            <p class="constraint-card__text">I worked with another designer and closely with the founder to move from findings to direction quickly.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection id="exploration" title="Exploration">
		<h2 class="section-tagline">The obvious fix wasn't enough.</h2>
		<p>I sketched several directions to make more songs easier to discover before moving into hi-fi.</p>
		<div class="observation">
    <span class="observation__label">01</span>
    <div class="observation__body">
        <p class="observation__title">Bigger arrows</p>
        <p class="observation__text">The smallest change, but they still relied on the same interaction users were already overlooking.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="observation">
    <span class="observation__label">02</span>
    <div class="observation__body">
        <p class="observation__title">Vertical feed</p>
        <p class="observation__text">More songs would be visible at once, but it competed with the page's existing vertical scroll.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="observation">
    <span class="observation__label">03</span>
    <div class="observation__body">
        <p class="observation__title">Horizontal Carousel</p>
        <p class="observation__text">It preserved the existing cards, separated browsing from page navigation, and matched the swipe behavior I was already seeing in UXCam.</p>
    </div>
</div>
		<img src="/images/lingotune/brainstorming.png" alt="..." class="case-study-image" />
		
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection id="decision" title="Decision">
		<h2 class="section-tagline"> I followed the behavior users were already showing me.</h2>
		<p>The horizontal carousel matched the swipe behavior I saw in UXCam, preserved the existing song cards, and kept browsing separate from the page’s vertical scroll.</p>
		<div class="observation">
    <span class="observation__label">01</span>
    <div class="observation__body">
        <p class="observation__title">Swipe for browsing</p>
        <p class="observation__text">How users browse.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="observation">
    <span class="observation__label">02</span>
    <div class="observation__body">
        <p class="observation__title">Peeking cards for discovering</p>
        <p class="observation__text">How they know there's more.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="observation">
    <span class="observation__label">03</span>
    <div class="observation__body">
        <p class="observation__title">Pagination for orientation</p>
        <p class="observation__text">How they know where they are.</p>
    </div>
</div>
		<img src="/images/lingotune/preview.png" alt="..." class="case-study-image case-study-image--phone" />
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection id="solution" title="Solution">
		<h3 class="solution-block-heading">01 Swipe to browse</h3>
		<p>Horizontal swipe matched the behavior users were already attempting in UXCam. Instead of finding a control first, they could browse with a gesture they already understood.</p>
		<img src="/images/lingotune/3designdecision.png" alt="..." class="case-study-image case-study-image--wide" />
		<h3 class="solution-block-heading">02 Show what's next</h3>
		<p>I exposed parts of the neighboring cards so users could see immediately that more songs existed. The layout itself became the cue to keep going.</p>
		<img src="/images/lingotune/1designdecision.png" alt="..." class="case-study-image case-study-image--wide" />
		<h3 class="solution-block-heading">03 Keep users oriented</h3>
		<p>Pagination gives lightweight feedback once users start browsing.</p>
		<p>The peeking cards answer "is there more?" The dots answer "where am I?"</p>
		<img src="/images/lingotune/2designdecision.png" alt="..." class="case-study-image case-study-image--wide" />
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection id="outcome" title="Outcome">
		<h2 class="section-tagline">The redesign shipped in the next release.</h2>
		<p>
			The team approved the direction, and my redesigned song browsing experience shipped in Lingotune's next release.
		</p>
		<p> While I didn't have access to post-launch metrics, if I continued measuring this release, I'd track: </p>
		<div class="observation">
    <span class="observation__label">01</span>
    <div class="observation__body">
        <p class="observation__title">Browse Depth</p>
        <p class="observation__text">Do more users move beyond the first song?</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="observation">
    <span class="observation__label">02</span>
    <div class="observation__body">
        <p class="observation__title">Lesson Starts</p>
        <p class="observation__text">Does browsing lead more users into lessons?</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="observation">
    <span class="observation__label">03</span>
    <div class="observation__body">
        <p class="observation__title">Screen Drop-Off</p>
        <p class="observation__text">Are fewer users leaving the app at this point?</p>
    </div>
</div>
		<img src="/images/lingotune/nishoutcome.png" alt="..." class="case-study-image case-study-image--wide" />
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection id="outcome" title="Reflection">
		<h2 class="section-tagline">What I learned!</h2>
		<p> Sometimes the answer is already in the <b> behavior. </b> </p>
		<p> The best UX often isn't inventing something new. It's noticing the mental models users already carry into your product and designing <b> with </b> them, not against them. </p>
	</CaseStudySection>

	
</CaseStudyLayout>